        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The Space Shuttle Atlantis launched in April carrying a crew of seven.  The crew consisted of Commander Michael Bloomfield, Pilot Stephen Frick, and Mission Specialists Ellen Ochoa, Jerry Ross, Rex Walheim, Steve Smith, and Lee Morin.  Mission Specialist Jerry Ross became the first human to be launched into space seven times, and Atlantis became the first Shuttle to use three block II main engines.  These engines have been redesigned to double their reliability and safety.
<P>
After docking with the station of April 10, 2002, the STS-110 crew performed four spacewalks and used the Shuttle and International Space Station (ISS) robotic arms to install and outfit the S-Zero (S0) truss.  The S0 truss is the first of nine pieces that will make up the station's external framework.  This was the first time that the station's robotic arm was used to maneuver spacewalkers around the station and the first time that all of a Shuttle crew's spacewalks were based out of the station's Quest Airlock.
<P>
Although the primary objective of the STS-110 mission was to deliver the S0 truss and other equipment, several life sciences experiments were also performed during this mission.  These experiments were:<BR>
<LI>Subregional Assessment of Bone Loss in the Axial Skeleton in Long-term Space Flight
<LI>Crewmember and Crew-Ground Interactions During International Space Station Missions
<LI>Effects of Altered Gravity on Spinal Cord Excitability (H-Reflex)
<LI>Effect of Microgravity on the Peripheral Subcutaneous Veno-arteriolar Reflex in Humans (Xenon1)
<LI>The Effects of EVA and Long-Term Exposure to Microgravity on Pulmonary Function (PuFF)
<LI>Renal Stone Risk during Space Flight: Assessment and Countermeasure Validation
<P>
Several Detailed Supplementary Objectives (DSOs) were also flown during the STS-110 mission.  DSOs are space and life sciences investigations.  Their purpose is to determine the extent of physiological deconditioning resulting from space flight, test countermeasures to those changes, and characterize the environment of the Space Shuttle and/or space station relative to crew health.  The DSOs that were performed during this mission were:<BR>
<LI>Monitoring Latent Virus Reactivation and Shedding in Astronauts
<LI>Individual Susceptibility to Post-Space Flight Orthostatic Intolerance
<LI>Space Flight and Immune Function
<LI>Space Flight-Induced Reactivation of Latent Epstein-Barr Virus
<LI>Test of Midodrine as a Counteractive Measure Against Postflight Orthostatic Hypotension
<P>
Detailed Test Objectives (DTOs) are experiments aimed at testing, evaluating or documenting Space Shuttle systems or hardware, or proposed improvements to the Space Shuttle or space station hardware, systems and operations.  DTOs flown on STS-110 were Shuttle Automatic Reboost Testing, Space Station RMS Dynamic Model Validation, Single-String Global Positioning System, and Crosswind Landing Performance.
<P>
Atlantis landed at Kennedy Space Center in Florida on April 19, 2002, concluding a successful mission.<END>
